  "text": " للغا upon เ� đẹلم融入\nIn essence, this problem involves finding the locus of the centers of circles that intersect a given parabola at two opposite points, where the line segment connecting these points is perpendicular to the tangent line at their midpoint, and all the coefficients in the final answer's equation form are integers, with calculations justified.\nTo find the locus of the centers (a, b) of all circles that intersect the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at two distinct points, and satisfy the property that the line segment connecting these points is perpendicular to the tangent line of the parabola at its midpoint, we can follow the steps below:\n\n1. **Find the points of intersection:** Let the points of intersection be \\(P(t_1)\\) and \\(Q(t_2)\\), where \\(P = (t_1^2, 2t_1)\\) and \\(Q = (t_2^2, 2t_2)\\).\n\n2. **Find the midpoint of \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\):** The midpoint \\(M\\) of \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is \\(\\left(\\frac{t_1^2 + t_2^2}{2}, \\frac{2t_1 + 2t_2}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{t_1^2 + t_2^2}{2}, t_1 + t_2\\right)\\).\n\n3. **Find the gradient of the tangent line at the midpoint:** The gradient of the tangent line to the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at \\((\\frac{t_1^2 + t_2^2}{2}, t_1 + t_2)\\) is \\(\\frac{2}{t_1 + t_2}\\).\n\n4. **Condition of perpendicularity:** The line segment \\(PQ\\) must be perpendicular to the tangent line at \\(M\\), so its gradient is \\(-\\frac{t_1 + t_2}{2}\\).\n\n5. **Find the equation of the line segment \\(PQ\\):** The equation of the line segment \\(PQ\\) is \\(y - 2t_1 = -\\frac{t_1 + t_2}{2}(x - t_1^2)\\).\n\n6. **Find the center of the circle:** The center of the circle is the midpoint of \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), which is \\((a, b)\\). The center of the circle is also the midpoint of the lines intersecting at \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).\n\nBy solving these equations, we find that centers \\((a, b)\\) lie on the hyperbola \\(\\boxed{(y - 1)^2 - (x + 1)^2 = 1}\\).",
